WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:05.470
Dracula has been haunting the big screen for
decades, and along the way, audiences have

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.400
seen numerous interpretations of the character,
some more effective than others.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.580
In the mood for some bloodsucking cinema?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.520
Since 1902's Voyage dans la Lune, filmmakers
have been shooting people into space.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.560
But as both characters and audiences of Dracula
3000 know, sometimes space bites.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.820
The movie follows a group of space salvagers
led by Captain Van Helsing.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.780
The team encounters a derelict ship where
they find the former captain, dead and clutching

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:33.840
a crucifix.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.320
Van Helsing and friends soon learn why as
they encounter Count Orlock, better known

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.520
as Nosferatu.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.560
This is where all the running, screaming,
and bleeding starts.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.440
"So what the hell is a vampire?"

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.320
But while vampires and space are exciting
on their own, Dracula 3000 leaves audiences

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.080
with one of the worst Alien ripoffs and the
single worst Dracula movie.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.160
Unlike other adaptations, Werner Herzog's
Nosferatu the Vampyre was a direct adaptation

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.000
of the original and aesthetically breaks from
most modern renditions.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.500
Instead of perfectly slicked-back hair and
a red cape, Klaus Kinski's Count Orlock dons

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:19.020
simple black garb, a bald scalp, pointed ears,
dark under-eye circles, and rodent-like teeth.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.640
Nosferatu the Vampyre is truly the stuff of
nightmares, thanks to the director's singular

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.840
perspective and striking directorial eye.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.320
3D cinema, like locusts, appears in cycles.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.920
In 2012, Dracula 3D premiered amidst the most
recent cycle of 3D, chewing its way through

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:36.420
the industry.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.030
But while Dracula 3D's release followed successful
3D horror ventures like My Bloody Valentine

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:49.400
3D, cult director Dario Argento's effort seems
hellbent on eroding any audience faith in

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.100
the technology.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.720
Soap opera-style camera work, low-quality
3D, and even lower quality CGI result in a

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.040
rough viewing experience.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.820
A handful of those present at the premiere
noted that the film walks the line of "so

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:02.500
bad it's good."

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:07.460
Maybe someday audience perception will tip
it from "bad" to "good", but for now Dracula

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.880
3D remains one of the worst Dracula films
ever made.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:17.080
Bela Lugosi's legacy lives on thanks in large
part to his utterly devastating turn in the

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.840
1931 film Dracula.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:24.480
Regarded by many as one of the best vampire
films of all time, Tod Browning's adaptation

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.060
is almost single-handedly responsible for
the vampire films audiences enjoy today.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:34.700
Lugosi was so adept at the part thanks to
his years of experience playing it onstage,

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.880
though Universal Studios almost didn't cast
him.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.520
Thanks to the hands of fate, audiences were
introduced to his singular presence.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.720
Though the cast outside of Lugosi is regarded
as mediocre, the film boasts luscious cinematography

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.020
and is held together by the sheer power of
the star's presence.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.500
Wes Craven's pioneering and typically exquisite
work as a writer, director and producer of

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.321
slasher films like Nightmare on Elm Street
and Scream earned him the title "the Master

00:03:02.321 --> 00:03:02.820
of Horror."

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:08.900
But Craven also hitched his name to a few
flops, with Dracula 2000 being the most embarrassing

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:09.400
example.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.740
Dracula 2000, like many films at the turn
of the millennium, sought to pull a classic

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.600
character into the 21st century.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.640
While vampires typically live forever, Dracula
probably would have been better off dead.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.370
Despite the absurd plot, complete with a time-traveling
Van Helsing, critics were forgiving of this

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.210
corpse-like revival of Stoker's classic given
Gerard Butler's pretty decent rendition of

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:32.760
Dracula.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:39.360
Hollywood, determined to get maximum bang
for its buck, sometimes reshot films in different

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:39.860
languages.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:46.200
Such is the case with the Spanish version
of the 1931 Dracula, which is just as good,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.220
if not better, than its English counterpart.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.200
Where Tod Browning's film relied heavily on
its star power and cinematography, director

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.020
George Melford's style was more visually dynamic.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.260
The crew also benefited from having the English
language dailies to learn and build from.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.460
Though it vanished for many years, a copy
surfaced in the 1970s.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:13.060
On October 25th, 2015, Spanish-language Drácula
made its official debut as a part of a Turner

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.080
Classic Movies' "TCM Presents" series and
redefined Dracula history forever.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.980
"She's alive?"

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.740
"She's Nosferatu."

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.880
"She's Italian?"

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:30.800
The line between horror and comedy can be
razor thin, leading many filmmakers into the

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:31.740
realm of parody.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:36.240
While a bad horror film can be funny, bad
parody is usually just bad.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.100
Mel Brooks' Dracula: Dead and Loving It could've
been the spiritual successor to Young Frankenstein.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.060
Most of the cast were veterans of Hollywood
comedy who should've been more than capable

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.900
of parodying Lugosi's 1931 version.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.500
But the ingredients just didn't come together.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.440
Thankfully, Mel Brooks' other films aren't
going anywhere if you're looking for a good

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:55.940
laugh.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.640
Other than Lugosi, the late Christopher Lee
is easily one of the best Draculas of all

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:02.140
time.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.980
Like many of the other Dracula films, Horror
of Dracula largely adheres to Bram Stoker's

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:08.480
text.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.660
But unlike many others, Horror of Dracula's
story lives up to its stellar cast, thanks

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.480
to a well-crafted script from Jimmy Sangster.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.580
Director Terence Fisher creates the requisite
mood, and the capable cast flushes out the

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:27.300
material beautifully, especially Peter Cushing,
who is arguably the best film Van Helsing

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:27.800
to date.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:32.640
Horror of Dracula established many of the
visual flourishes expected from a vampire

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.160
film: prominent fangs, glowing red eyes, and
pre-made wooden stakes.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:44.080
Through it all, Lee oozes charisma and terror
in equal measure, which resulted in typecasting

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.220
for much of his career.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.860
Unlike Lugosi, Lee eventually broke from Dracula
and is remembered for his other illustrious

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:51.360
roles.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.000
Dracula fans will be forgiven for missing
this lesser-known production co-created by

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.900
WGBH Boston and BBC Wales.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:05.000
But Masterpiece Theatre should be familiar
to public television fans, having been responsible

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.360
for some incredible literary adaptations.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.940
From a production perspective, Dracula's pristine
period setting should make it an easy fit.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.540
However, this Dracula takes a few creative
liberties, such as omitting certain well-known

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.560
characters and introducing a more explicit
disease subplot.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.200
The changes, though novel, do more to alienate
literary fans than give the story new life.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:33.260
Overall the film struggles to find its voice,
landing somewhere between typical Masterpiece

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.520
artistry and popcorn-flick throwaway.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.140
All adaptations owe a great debt to the 1921
classic Nosferatu.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.780
This premier Dracula film introduced the character
and vampire mythology to a wider audience.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.120
The great irony is that Nosferatu had to pretend
it wasn't a Dracula film.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:57.300
The story goes that studio co-founder Albin
Grau commissioned screenwriter Henrik Galeen

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.940
to pen a production based on Bram Stoker's
novel.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:05.040
But when the Stoker estate refused to grant
permission, the script was reworked to avoid

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.080
copyright infringement.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.420
It didn't work, and the studio filed for bankruptcy
soon after the film's release.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.030
All copies of the film were to be destroyed
by a court order, but thanks to international

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.240
distribution, Nosferatu survived.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.780
So has its ability to linger, hauntingly,
agelessly, like Dracula himself.

